            <ArticleTitle>[Both-sided iliac vein thrombosis in a 18-year old patient with lumbago].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>228-31</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00108-007-1992-9</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A MRI of the lumbal spine was performed on a patient with lumbago. Besides the fact of no evidence for a myelopathy caused by a herniated disc a both-sided iliac vein thrombosis could be seen. In further examination the coincidence of a congenital absence of vena cava inferior with distinctive collateral vessel systems over lumbal and spinal veins and heterozygosis for Factor V Leiden abnormality could be identified as cause for thrombosis. It is very likely that symptoms developed as a result of spinal vein hyperplasia and consecutive myelon compression.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
